sixty thousand tons  of toxic waste  this whole area is extremely hazardous  and it's been sitting here bubbling away  for decades  this is an acid tar lagoon a swamp  made up of unmanaged dumped mess from  oil refineries near chester  just over here we'll find the acid tar  lagoon local councilor matt bryan  contacted us  to highlight these pernicious pits and  their blight  on his constituency it's a toxic waste  dump it's absolutely vile to be around  here  there's nothing living in there anything  that's close to  being alive is covered in oil and tar  and it's just a  generally disgusting place to be and as  you can see  it's bubbling the smell is awful um  and there's oil leaching out into the  ground around us  in the late 1970s dumping this kind of  industrial waste  was outlawed but before that disused  quarries were being filled up  with thousands of tons of poisonous  gunge and they're still here today  the environment agency claimed that the  clay bund holds it all in and as you've  seen  on the outskirts of the site even as far  as 100 meters away  we've got deep pools of tyre  which isn't good for the environment  whichever way you look at it this is the  legacy of bad decision making from the  1960s  and we've got a responsibility to sort  it out  this site is far from unique it's just  one part  of britain's dirty secret according to  the environment agency there are only  two  acetal lagoons in this country where our  investigation found  councils across the uk have officially  identified 34  and records show only a handful of them  are marked as hazardous but we can  reveal  there are many harmful toxic pits like  this  that have not been recorded and they  could be at least 150  across the country many of the sites  we've identified are in the north of  england  one in blackpool four in the amber  valley district of derbyshire alone  and another 18 recorded across northern  ireland  stopping the dumping only paused the  problem  the toxic waste already discarded hasn't  been cleaned up  in the 1980s a lagoon in wales was so  flammable  it burned for days when it was  accidentally set on fire  many properties of these pits are  similar we took samples  to see how dangerous they really are  hi nz if you pop that on we can go into  the lab  dr will meredith is an environmental  scientist at the university of  nottingham  he specializes in petrochemicals i've  added a small amount of the tar  into this vial and added a bit of water  and i'm now just going to test it with  this indicator paper  uh this is litmus which will indicate  the ph and as you can see  it goes to a bright red color indicating  a gauge of one to two  so this is a highly acidic material how  dangerous  is this acid i mean it's nasty it's  um it's going to be highly corrosive  it's not the sort of material that you  want to be exposed to  to get on your skin or on your clothes  the science shows  the impacts of acid lagoons have been  devastating  and they remain dangerous it's obviously  very damaging  locally from an environmental  perspective certainly from a wildlife  it's so acidic that things cannot grow  on it  without assistance and also if people  come into touch with it  into contact with it you know you can  get burns you can get corrosion  you can get damage it's our  responsibility as a community i think  to uh to to protect each other from  these  uh legacy sites of poorer environmental  standards in the past  oh look at that  it absolutely stinks and that's just  pure oil for years acitar lagoons have  been a hazard to our natural environment  controlled waters and in some instances  human health  particularly with people living nearby  i had no idea that it was here you don't  know much about them i feel like that is  a tad bit of a worry that you're not  told about them you're not made aware of  it so it's almost like it's trying to be  hidden in a way it's not made publicized  and the fact that there are so many  across the country  you know you would obviously prefer for  them to be treated so it makes it better  for the environment and you know local  wildlife  i mean i've been here for four years now  and i'm not don't know much  too much about it uh obviously i've got  kids so  the environment for the health of the  kids it's obviously not great being  there  it's literally a few hundred yards down  the road so  um it's not great for anyone that lives  in this area at all  having poisoned the environment for  decades cleaning them up now will cost  tens of millions of pounds some of the  sites are so  toxic there are no facilities in europe  large enough  to decontaminate them many of the  companies who once owned the land and  dumped the waste  are long gone today no one is taking  responsibility  the environment agency told us the  identification of these sites  sits with local authorities however when  asked we will work collaboratively to  inspect the land to ensure risks to the  environment are understood  and the area remains safe we've also  published technical guidance on waste  acid tars  to ensure these sites can be managed in  a way which limits  impact on the environment and  surrounding areas  [Music]  this is what is cleaning up the mess  we're now getting  the roots into the ground and into the  tower  for many years dr harvey wood has been  investigating  acid lagoons at this known site in derby  he's worked to try and make it safe but  is concerned  the national issue is too big the  environmental health departments across  the country  would rather not recognize these  because they do not know  how to go about the cleanup because they  see it as an open-ended  liability cost-wise and  no local authority is going to have that  kind of facility  to enable the cleanup and  no one who may own the land would like  to have that liability either  but dr wood has a solution and one that  works  this area used to be an acetal lagoon  but a sustainable  and very natural process means the toxic  waste  is no more we wanted to plant willows on  the  site because we know willows like acid  conditions  we covered the tar with a layer of  spent mushroom compost we then planted  the willows  and they've rooted into the tar down to  18 inches to two feet of the tar  in a lot of the areas and that is now no  longer acid  dr wood now hopes this process can be  used across the country  at other hazardous lagoons a cheaper  more eco-friendly fix  to these dangerous dormant sites  [Music]  acids are lagoons may have been around  for more than half a century  but still so little is known and being  done  about our irresponsibilities of the past  and for a nation  that is committed to be a world leader  on climate change  accepting this responsibility and  clearing up the mess of historical  actions  will no doubt help that cause

